    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>DaVinci Resolve is unique in the editing world — it's the only professional-grade NLE that offers a <strong>genuinely free version</strong> with no watermark, no time limit, and no feature-crippling. But DaVinci Resolve Studio, priced at <strong>$295 USD (₹24,900 in India)</strong> as a one-time perpetual license, unlocks a specific set of AI and hardware-accelerated tools. This guide breaks down exactly what you get, what you don't, and whether you need to upgrade.</p>

    <h2>Quick Answer (Featured Snippet)</h2>
    <p>DaVinci Resolve Free includes the complete editor, color grading (Color page), Fusion VFX, and Fairlight audio — enough for most YouTubers and content creators. DaVinci Resolve Studio ($295 one-time in the US; <strong>₹31,980 officially listed on Blackmagic's India store, excluding GST/Octroi</strong> — so the actual out-the-door cost is closer to <strong>₹37,700 with 18% GST</strong>) adds Neural Engine AI tools (Magic Mask, Super Scale, Face Refinement), GPU noise reduction, HDR grading, multi-GPU rendering, resolutions/frame rates up to 120fps at 32K, and Blackmagic Cloud collaboration.</p>

    <blockquote>
        <strong>Price verification note:</strong> Several third-party sites (Techjockey, Miracamp, etc.) list outdated figures like ₹24,900 or ₹28,980. The number above (₹31,980, GST-exclusive) is pulled directly from Blackmagic Design's official India store page — treat that as the authoritative figure and always double-check on blackmagicdesign.com/in before publishing a price in your video.
    </blockquote>

