    <h2>Common Myth Busted</h2>
    <p><strong>Myth:</strong> "Free version is a trial or limited version."<br>
    <strong>Fact:</strong> It is not a trial. No expiry date, no export limit, no watermark, no feature that degrades over time. Studio is an add-on layer, not an unlock of a restricted version.</p>

    <h2>Quick FAQ</h2>
    <p><strong>Q: Does DaVinci Resolve Free add a watermark?</strong><br>
    No — exports are completely clean, unlike CapCut or Filmora free tiers.</p>

    <p><strong>Q: Is DaVinci Resolve Studio a subscription?</strong><br>
    No — it's a one-time perpetual license. Major version updates (e.g., Resolve 20 to 21) have historically been included free, though Blackmagic hasn't ruled out charging for a future major upgrade.</p>

    <p><strong>Q: What's the DaVinci Resolve Studio price in India?</strong><br>
    Officially listed at <strong>₹31,980</strong> on Blackmagic Design's India store — this figure includes customs duty but explicitly excludes GST and Octroi. With 18% GST added, the real-world cost is approximately <strong>₹37,700</strong>. Third-party resellers list anywhere from ₹28,980 to ₹35,376+ depending on GST handling and markup — always verify against blackmagicdesign.com/in before quoting a number on camera.</p>

    <p><strong>Q: Do I need Studio for color grading?</strong><br>
    No — full node-based color grading, scopes, qualifiers, and Power Windows are all in the free version.</p>

    




